ed kingdom we  wouldn't be talking about  millions of extra uh pounds of money for  welsh  businesses if it wasn't for the fact  that we are part of  that big insurance policy that is the  united kingdom  paying in when we can drawing out when  we need it  i think wales has the best of both  worlds a strong and assertive devolution  settlement and membership of what i want  to see as a successful united kingdom  you must be frustrated though um i was  chatting to andy burnham uh  in earlier on in this hour actually mr  eckford and and he was basically saying  the north feels like there's  second-class citizens  to what's happening in westminster do  the people of wales feel the same  well what i think i've been frustrated  about is the fact that we have not had  throughout the coronavirus period a  sufficiently regular and reliable  pattern of engagement with the uk  government  we've the last cobra meeting we had is  nearly three weeks ago  i asked then for another meeting to  discuss sage's advice about a fire break  period  the first minister of scotland wrote  immediately to support that call  yet three weeks have gone by without a  meeting of that sort happening  that's my frustration that the uk  government in whose hands this  lies have not acted to bring us together  around the table to share information  have those discussions make better  decisions  with the reliability of the regularity  that i think would have  led to a stronger set of understanding  and a stronger set of decisions  that has been frustrating and remains so  how effective has the fire break been i  know there's another week or so  to go eight days to go but um have you  got any indication of how effective it's  been  well we've only got very early  indications we won't know whether the r  number has come down as we wished it to  be we won't know whether we've stemmed  the flow of people into hospitals  for a week or two after the fire break  ends but we do have  early figures of travel in